                    <history>
                        <origin><origDate calendar="#Gregorian" notBefore="1200" notAfter="1299">s.
                            xiii</origDate></origin>
                        <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_795">Mettingham,
                            Suffolk, Collegiate church of St Mary the Virgin</orgName> <p>This
                            attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of
                            Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the Supplement (1987). The
                            corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index card records the
                            following information, which was deemed unsatisfactory for an
                            attribution: <q>Ex dono <persName role="dnr">Willelmi Pepyr</persName>
                            quondam vicarii de Metyngham cuius anime propicietur deus Amen</q> (fol.
                            10v, early s. xv). The kalendar has the dedication of Norwich cathedral
                            as an early addition (fo. 4r) and the later addition of William Pepyr's
                            obit (fo. 7v). Blomefield notes that William Pepyr was instituted vicar
                            in 1377 (fo. 10v).</p></provenance>
                        <provenance type="MGLB3_laterOwners">Kalendar contains in original hand in
                            red <q>Obitus magistri Johannis de Holebrok militis a.d. Mᵒ CCᵐᵒ
                            nono</q> (fol. 3v).</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MGLB3_laterOwners"><q><persName role="fmo">Fran:
                            Blomfield</persName> Coll.: Gon: Caii Camb: 17--</q> nuper de Fersfield
                            Com: Norf: (fol. 2r). Also notes by him and his bookplate
                            1736.</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MGLB3_laterOwners">Thomas Martin sale 18-21 March 1774,
                            lot 247.</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MGLB3_laterOwners">George Scott sale Sotheby 12 March 1781
                            lot 90 to Jackson (67/).</provenance>
                    </history>
